From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] A navigation bar the reader put away answers only a swipe up from the bottom edge

The navigation bar is shown by default on this model, and the reader owns its visibility:
a tap on the bar's own key (patch 0077) puts it away, and a swipe up from the bottom edge
brings it back for good. Two states, no timers.

The state is the Settings.Global entry `readom_nav_locked` (1 = put away). It is a literal
name, not a new constant in `Settings.java`: a new constant would change framework.jar and
the ART boot image with it, and every application in /data would be recompiled at the next
upgrade. Hiding is still `policy_control = immersive.navigation=*`, written by the same
toggle, so the two settings agree and the state survives a restart.

While the bar is put away, two things must not bring it back:

  - a swipe down from the top. In AOSP `requestTransientBars()` computes
    `sb = mStatusBarController.checkShowTransientBarLw()` and
    `nb = mNavigationBarController.checkShowTransientBarLw()` and shows whichever is true,
    no matter which edge was swiped -- the top swipe asks for the status bar and the
    navigation bar came out with it ("the status bar and the navigation bar come out
    together", the reader's words). One condition is added to `nb`, and the existing guard
    in `requestTransientBars()` refuses navigation-bar requests outright while the bar is
    put away;

  - the power-button panic and any other transient request, which go through
    `transientNavBarAllowed` and are refused while the bar is put away.

The reader's way back is `onSwipeFromBottom()`: while the bar is put away it writes 0 (and
drops `policy_control`), and the bar is simply shown -- no peek, no timer, nothing that
takes it away again.

A boot without this patch ignores `readom_nav_locked` and behaves as it always did.

Not run on a device yet when this patch was written; how to run it is in
`readom-port/reports/t09-nav-impl.md`.

